x-efficient wealth strategies.\",\"knowsAbout\":[\"Federal Income Tax\",\"State Income Tax\",\"FICA Tax\",\"Payroll Tax\",\"IRS Compliance\",\"Tax Planning\",\"Multi-State Taxation\",\"Tax-Advantaged Accounts\"],\"sameAs\":[\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/rachelmitchellcpa/\",\"https://www.aicpa-cima.com/\"],\"worksFor\":{\"@type\":\"Organization\",\"name\":\"TheTaxCalc\",\"url\":\"https://thetaxcalc.com\"},\"image\":\"https://thetaxcalc.com/author-rachel-mitchell.webp\"},{\"@id\":\"https://thetaxcalc.com/blog/texas-tax-guide-2026#article\",\"@type\":\"Article\",\"headline\":\"Texas Tax Guide 2026: No Income Tax, But What About Property and Sales Tax?\",\"description\":\"Texas has no state income tax, but property tax is among the highest. Complete guide to Texas taxes in 2026.\",\"image\":{\"@type\":\"ImageObject\",\"url\":\"https://thetaxcalc.com/opengraph-image.png\",\"width\":1200,\"height\":630},\"datePublished\":\"2026-06-19T12:00:00.000Z\",\"dateModified\":\"2026-06-19T12:00:00.000Z\",\"author\":{\"@id\":\"https://thetaxcalc.com/blog/texas-tax-guide-2026#author\"},\"publisher\":{\"@id\":\"https://thetaxcalc.com/#organization\"},\"mainEntityOfPage\":{\"@type\":\"WebPage\",\"@id\":\"https://thetaxcalc.com/blog/texas-tax-guide-2026\"},\"keywords\":[\"texas tax calculator\",\"income tax in texas\",\"taxable income\",\"tax returns\",\"property tax rate\",\"sales tax\"],\"wordCount\":2188,\"articleSection\":\"State Tax\"},{\"@id\":\"https://thetaxcalc.com/blog/texas-tax-guide-2026#breadcrumb\",\"@type\":\"BreadcrumbList\",\"itemListElement\":[{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":1,\"name\":\"Home\",\"item\":\"https://thetaxcalc.com\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":2,\"name\":\"Blog\",\"item\":\"https://thetaxcalc.com/blog\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":3,\"name\":\"Texas Tax Guide 2026: No Income Tax, But What About Property and Sales Tax?\",\"item\":\"https://thetaxcalc.com/blog/texas-tax-guide-2026\"}]}]}</script><nav aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\" class=\"mb-6 flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm text-muted-foreground\"><a class=\"hover:text-foreground transition-colors\" href=\"/\">Home</a><span class=\"text-muted-foreground\" aria-hidden=\"true\">/</span><a class=\"hover:text-foreground transition-colors\" href=\"/blog\">Blog</a><span class=\"text-muted-foreground\" aria-hidden=\"true\">/</span><span class=\"truncate text-foreground font-medium max-w-[200px] sm:max-w-none\">Texas Tax Guide 2026: No Income Tax, But What About Property and Sales Tax?</span></nav><article class=\"mb-8\"><div class=\"mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-4 mb-8\"><div class=\"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2\"><span class=\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium bg-violet-100 text-violet-800 dark:bg-violet-500/20 dark:text-violet-400 border-violet-300 dark:border-violet-500/30\">State Tax</span><span class=\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-amber-300 bg-amber-100 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-amber-800 dark:border-amber-500/30 dark:bg-amber-500/20 dark:text-amber-400\">Featured</span></div><h1 class=\"text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-foreground sm:text-4xl leading-tight\">Texas Tax Guide 2026: No Income Tax, But What About Property and Sales Tax?</h1><p class=\"mt-2 text-base text-muted-foreground\">A CPA-reviewed guide by Rachel Mitchell, CPA — updated for 2026 tax year</p><p class=\"text-lg text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed\">Texas has no state income tax, but property tax is among the highest. Complete guide to Texas taxes in 2026.</p><div class=\"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 text-sm text-muted-foreground border-b border-border/30 pb-4\"><span>By <!-- -->Rachel Mitchell<!-- -->, <!-- -->CPA</span><span><time dateTime=\"2026-06-19T12:00:00.000Z\">June 19, 2026</time></span><span>11<!-- --> min read</span></div><div class=\"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2\"><span class=\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/50 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\">texas tax calculator</span><span class=\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/50 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\">income tax in texas</span><span class=\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/50 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\">taxable income</span><span class=\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/50 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\">tax returns</span><span class=\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/50 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\">property tax rate</span><span class=\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/50 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\">sales tax</span></div></div><div class=\"mx-auto max-w-5xl lg:flex lg:gap-8\"><aside class=\"hidden lg:block lg:w-64 lg:shrink-0\"><div class=\"sticky top-24\"><nav aria-label=\"Table of contents\" class=\"space-y-1\"><p class=\"text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground mb-3\">On this page</p><ul class=\"space-y-1 text-sm\"><li><a href=\"#no-state-income-tax-in-texas-what-it-means\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">No State Income Tax in Texas: What It Means</a></li><li><a href=\"#texas-property-tax-among-the-highest-in-the-nation\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Texas Property Tax: Among the Highest in the Nation</a></li><li><a href=\"#what-this-means-in-real-dollars\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">What This Means in Real Dollars</a></li><li><a href=\"#texas-sales-tax-6-25-state-local-additions\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Texas Sales Tax: 6.25% State + Local Additions</a></li><li><a href=\"#what-s-taxable-in-texas\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">What&#x27;s Taxable in Texas?</a></li><li><a href=\"#understanding-taxable-income-in-texas\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Understanding Taxable Income in Texas</a></li><li><a href=\"#filing-tax-returns-in-texas\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Filing Tax Returns in Texas</a></li><li><a href=\"#the-franchise-tax-texas-s-business-tax\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">The Franchise Tax: Texas&#x27;s Business Tax</a></li><li><a href=\"#how-texas-compares-to-other-states\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">How Texas Compares to Other States</a></li><li><a href=\"#texas-vs-california\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Texas vs. California</a></li><li><a href=\"#texas-vs-new-york\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Texas vs. New York</a></li><li><a href=\"#the-bottom-line-on-texas-taxes\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">The Bottom Line on Texas Taxes</a></li><li><a href=\"#texas-income-tax-the-complete-picture\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Texas Income Tax: The Complete Picture</a></li><li><a href=\"#texas-income-tax-rate-2025-vs-2026\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Texas Income Tax Rate 2025 vs 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"#how-much-is-your-salary-after-taxes-in-texas\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">How Much Is Your Salary After Taxes in Texas?</a></li><li><a href=\"#100-000-after-taxes-in-texas\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">$100,000 After Taxes in Texas</a></li><li><a href=\"#200-000-after-taxes-in-texas\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">$200,000 After Taxes in Texas</a></li><li><a href=\"#other-common-salaries-after-taxes-in-texas\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Other Common Salaries After Taxes in Texas</a></li><li><a href=\"#25-an-hour-after-taxes-in-texas\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">$25 an Hour After Taxes in Texas</a></li><li><a href=\"#texas-sales-tax-rates-cars-and-permits\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Texas Sales Tax: Rates, Cars, and Permits</a></li><li><a href=\"#texas-sales-tax-rate\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Texas Sales Tax Rate</a></li><li><a href=\"#texas-sales-tax-on-cars\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Texas Sales Tax on Cars</a></li><li><a href=\"#texas-sales-tax-permit-and-login\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Texas Sales Tax Permit and Login</a></li><li><a href=\"#texas-sales-tax-exemption\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Texas Sales Tax Exemption</a></li><li><a href=\"#texas-sales-tax-rate-2025-vs-2026\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Texas Sales Tax Rate 2025 vs 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"#frequently-asked-questions-about-texas-taxes\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Frequently Asked Questions About Texas Taxes</a></li><li><a href=\"#does-texas-have-income-tax\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Does Texas have income tax?</a></li><li><a href=\"#what-is-the-income-tax-rate-in-texas\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">What is the income tax rate in Texas?</a></li><li><a href=\"#how-much-is-100-000-taxed-in-texas\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">How much is $100,000 taxed in Texas?</a></li><li><a href=\"#is-tax-8-25-in-texas\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Is tax 8.25% in Texas?</a></li><li><a href=\"#how-much-tax-is-on-100-in-texas\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">How much tax is on $100 in Texas?</a></li><li><a href=\"#what-is-the-sales-tax-on-a-30-000-car-in-texas\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">What is the sales tax on a $30,000 car in Texas?</a></li><li><a href=\"#how-do-texas-taxes-compare-to-other-states\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">How do Texas taxes compare to other states?</a></li></ul></nav></div></aside><div class=\"mx-auto max-w-3xl lg:mx-0 lg:flex-1\"><div class=\"prose-container\"><p>Everyone knows Texas has no income tax. It's practically a marketing slogan at this point. But if you're thinking about moving to Texas — or you already live there and want to understand your full tax picture — you need to look beyond the income tax line. Texas compensates for the lack of income tax with some of the highest property taxes in the country.</p><p>Let me break down exactly what you'll pay in Texas, from property tax to sales tax to everything in between.</p><h2 id=\"no-state-income-tax-in-texas-what-it-means\">No State Income Tax in Texas: What It Means</h2><p>Texas is one of nine states with no personal income tax. That means zero state tax on your wages, self-employment income, investment income, retirement distributions, or capital gains. There is no state income tax to file, no state tax return to worry about (for personal income), and no state withholding from your paycheck.</p><p>For someone earning $75,000, this translates to savings of roughly $3,000 to $4,000 compared to states like California or New York. On a $150,000 salary, the savings can exceed $8,000 per year.</p><p>But before you celebrate, let's look at where Texas makes up that revenue.</p><h2 id=\"texas-property-tax-among-the-highest-in-the-nation\">Texas Property Tax: Among the Highest in the Nation</h2><p>Texas property tax rates are the seventh-highest in the United States, with an average effective rate of approximately 1.60% of your home's assessed value. Some counties are even higher, pushing past 2.2%.</p><h3 id=\"what-this-means-in-real-dollars\">What This Means in Real Dollars</h3><div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table><thead><tr><th>Home Value</th><th>Annual Property Tax (avg 1.60%)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>$200,000</td><td>$3,200</td></tr><tr><td>$350,000</td><td>$5,600</td></tr><tr><td>$500,000</td><td>$8,000</td></tr><tr><td>$750,000</td><td>$12,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>If you're coming from a state like California (0.71% average effective rate), the property tax difference can be shocking. On a $500,000 home, you'd pay about $3,550 in California property tax versus $8,000 in Texas — a difference of $4,450 per year.</p><p>For renters, this is less of an issue since you don't directly pay property tax. But landlords pass these costs through in rent, so you're still paying indirectly.</p><p>Use our <a href=\"/texas-tax-calculator\">Texas Tax Calculator</a> to see your complete tax picture including property tax analysis.</p><h2 id=\"texas-sales-tax-6-25-state-local-additions\">Texas Sales Tax: 6.25% State + Local Additions</h2><p>Texas charges a 6.25% state sales tax, and local jurisdictions can add up to 2%, making the maximum combined rate 8.25%. The average combined rate across Texas is about 8.19%.</p><h3 id=\"what-s-taxable-in-texas\">What's Taxable in Texas?</h3><p>Most retail purchases are subject to sales tax, but there are some important exemptions:</p><ul><li><strong>Groceries</strong>: Most unprepared food items are exempt</li><li><strong>Prescription drugs</strong>: No sales tax</li><li><strong>Over-the-counter medicines</strong>: Taxable</li><li><strong>Clothing</strong>: Fully taxable (unlike some states with clothing exemptions)</li></ul><p>If you spend $30,000 per year on taxable goods and services in Texas at an average 8.19% rate, you're paying about $2,457 in sales tax annually.</p><h2 id=\"understanding-taxable-income-in-texas\">Understanding Taxable Income in Texas</h2><p>Since Texas has no state income tax, your taxable income is only relevant for federal tax purposes. Your federal taxable income is calculated by taking your gross income and subtracting either the standard deduction or itemized deductions.</p><p>For 2026, the federal standard deduction is $16,100 for single filers and $32,200 for married filing jointly. Even though Texas doesn't tax your income, you still need to file federal tax returns and pay federal income tax.</p><h2 id=\"filing-tax-returns-in-texas\">Filing Tax Returns in Texas</h2><p>Texans file federal tax returns like everyone else, but the process is simpler because there's no state return to file. You'll need:</p><ol><li><strong>Federal Form 1040</strong>: Standard federal tax return</li><li><strong>No state income tax return</strong>: Texas doesn't require one</li><li><strong>Property tax</strong>: Paid to your local county tax assessor (not on your tax return)</li><li><strong>Franchise tax</strong>: Only for businesses, not individuals</li></ol><p>This simplicity is one of the real benefits of living in Texas. No state tax return means less paperwork, fewer chances for errors, and no state tax deadline to worry about.</p><h2 id=\"the-franchise-tax-texas-s-business-tax\">The Franchise Tax: Texas's Business Tax</h2><p>If you own a business in Texas, you may be subject to the Texas franchise tax — sometimes called the \"margin tax.\" This is not an income tax on individuals, but a tax on business entities. The rates range from 0.331% to 0.75% on your business margins, depending on your revenue and industry.</p><p>Most small businesses with revenue under $2.47 million are exempt from the franchise tax. But if your business grows, this is something to plan for.</p><h2 id=\"how-texas-compares-to-other-states\">How Texas Compares to Other States</h2><p>The total tax burden in Texas — combining property tax, sales tax, and other taxes — averages around 8.22% of income, according to the Tax Foundation. This puts Texas in the middle of the pack nationally.</p><h3 id=\"texas-vs-california\">Texas vs. California</h3><div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tax Type</th><th>Texas</th><th>California</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Income Tax</td><td>0%</td><td>1% – 13.3%</td></tr><tr><td>Property Tax (avg)</td><td>1.60%</td><td>0.71%</td></tr><tr><td>Sales Tax (avg)</td><td>8.19%</td><td>8.82%</td></tr><tr><td>Total Burden</td><td>~8.22%</td><td>~11.49%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>On a $100,000 salary with a $350,000 home, Texas is typically $5,000–$7,000 cheaper per year than California, even with higher property taxes. Use our <a href=\"/compare/texas-vs-california\">comparison tool</a> to see the exact numbers for your situation.</p><h3 id=\"texas-vs-new-york\">Texas vs. New York</h3><p>The comparison with New York is even more dramatic. New York's income tax alone can exceed $6,000 on a $100,000 salary, plus NYC residents pay additional city taxes. Texas saves the average New Yorker $8,000–$12,000+ per year.</p><h2 id=\"the-bottom-line-on-texas-taxes\">The Bottom Line on Texas Taxes</h2><p>Texas is genuinely a low-tax state, but not as dramatically as the \"no income tax\" slogan suggests. The property tax is significant, and if you own an expensive home, it can eat into your income tax savings.</p><p><strong>Texas makes the most sense if:</strong></p><ul><li>You're a renter (you avoid the property tax entirely)</li><li>You have a high income but a modest home</li><li>You're a business owner who can benefit from no state income tax</li><li>You're retiring and want to protect your retirement income</li></ul><p><strong>Texas may not be ideal if:</strong></p><ul><li>You own an expensive home relative to your income</li><li>You live in an area with very high property tax rates (some suburbs exceed 2.5%)</li><li>You rely on public services that are funded by property taxes</li></ul><p>Use our <a href=\"/texas-tax-calculator\">Texas Tax Calculator</a> to see exactly what you'll pay, including property tax analysis and cost-of-living comparisons.</p><h2 id=\"texas-income-tax-the-complete-picture\">Texas Income Tax: The Complete Picture</h2><p>When people search for \"texas income tax,\" they often find conflicting information. Let's be crystal clear: <strong>Texas does not have a personal state income tax.</strong> There is no texas income tax rate, no texas income tax brackets, and no texas income tax return to file for individuals.</p><p>The Texas income tax percentage for individuals is exactly 0%. This has been the case since Texas constitutional amendment in 1993, which actually makes it extremely difficult to ever implement a state income tax.</p><h3 id=\"texas-income-tax-rate-2025-vs-2026\">Texas Income Tax Rate 2025 vs 2026</h3><p>Whether you're looking at texas income tax rate 2025 or texas income tax rate 2026, the answer is the same: 0%. Unlike other states that adjust their brackets annually, Texas has no brackets to adjust. The texas income tax brackets 2026 simply don't exist for individuals.</p><p>However, businesses in Texas do pay the franchise tax (sometimes called the margin tax), which functions similarly to an income tax for business entities. Use a texas income tax calculator (like ours) to estimate your total tax burden — which will include federal tax, FICA, property tax, and sales tax, but zero state income tax.</p><h2 id=\"how-much-is-your-salary-after-taxes-in-texas\">How Much Is Your Salary After Taxes in Texas?</h2><p>Here are the most common salary questions about Texas taxes, answered with real numbers:</p><h3 id=\"100-000-after-taxes-in-texas\">$100,000 After Taxes in Texas</h3><p>On a $100,000 salary in Texas (single filer), your take-home pay is approximately <strong>$75,600</strong>. Here's the breakdown:</p><ul><li>Federal income tax: ~$14,001</li><li>FICA (Social Security + Medicare): ~$7,649</li><li>Texas state income tax: $0</li><li><strong>Total tax</strong>: ~$21,650 (21.7% effective rate)</li></ul><h3 id=\"200-000-after-taxes-in-texas\">$200,000 After Taxes in Texas</h3><p>On $200,000 in Texas, you take home approximately <strong>$141,300</strong>. Texas saves you roughly $10,000–$15,000 compared to California or New York on this salary.</p><h3 id=\"other-common-salaries-after-taxes-in-texas\">Other Common Salaries After Taxes in Texas</h3><div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table><thead><tr><th>Salary</th><th>Take-Home Pay</th><th>Effective Tax Rate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>$60,000 after taxes Texas</td><td>~$48,500</td><td>19.2%</td></tr><tr><td>$70,000 after taxes Texas</td><td>~$55,600</td><td>20.6%</td></tr><tr><td>$120,000 after taxes Texas</td><td>~$92,500</td><td>22.9%</td></tr><tr><td>$150,000 after taxes Texas</td><td>~$112,800</td><td>24.8%</td></tr><tr><td>$250,000 after taxes Texas</td><td>~$180,200</td><td>27.9%</td></tr><tr><td>$300,000 after taxes Texas</td><td>~$212,000</td><td>29.3%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For a precise calculation based on your specific situation (401k contributions, filing status, etc.), use our <a href=\"/texas-tax-calculator\">Texas Tax Calculator</a> which works as a Dallas income tax calculator, Houston tax calculator, or any Texas city.</p><h3 id=\"25-an-hour-after-taxes-in-texas\">$25 an Hour After Taxes in Texas</h3><p>At $25/hour (approximately $52,000/year), your take-home pay in Texas is roughly $42,400 after federal tax and FICA. Near Houston TX or any other Texas city, the result is the same since there's no local income tax.</p><h2 id=\"texas-sales-tax-rates-cars-and-permits\">Texas Sales Tax: Rates, Cars, and Permits</h2><h3 id=\"texas-sales-tax-rate\">Texas Sales Tax Rate</h3><p>The Texas sales tax rate is 6.25% at the state level, with local jurisdictions adding up to 2% for a maximum combined rate of 8.25%. The average combined rate across Texas is approximately 8.19%.</p><p><strong>Is all of Texas 8.25% sales tax?</strong> No. The 8.25% is the maximum combined rate, but not all areas charge the full 2% local add-on. Some areas have lower combined rates, while major cities like Dallas, Houston, and San Antonio typically hit the 8.25% maximum.</p><h3 id=\"texas-sales-tax-on-cars\">Texas Sales Tax on Cars</h3><p>Texas sales tax on cars is 6.25% (the state rate only — no local add-ons for motor vehicles). On a $30,000 car, you'd pay $1,875 in sales tax. Texas also charges a standard presumptive value (SPV) tax on used car purchases from private parties, which may result in a different tax amount.</p><h3 id=\"texas-sales-tax-permit-and-login\">Texas Sales Tax Permit and Login</h3><p>If you're a business owner, you'll need a texas sales tax permit from the Texas Comptroller. You can:</p><ul><li>Apply for a texas sales tax permit online at the Comptroller's website</li><li>File returns via texas sales tax webfile (the state's online filing system)</li><li>Access the texas sales tax login at comptroller.texas.gov</li><li>Use the texas sales tax lookup tool to verify rates by location</li></ul><h3 id=\"texas-sales-tax-exemption\">Texas Sales Tax Exemption</h3><p>Texas offers sales tax exemptions for:</p><ul><li>Groceries (most unprepared food)</li><li>Prescription drugs</li><li>Manufacturing equipment</li><li>100% disabled veterans (vehicle sales tax exemption)</li><li>Agricultural items</li></ul><p>You can file a texas sales tax exemption form (Form 01-339) to make tax-exempt purchases for qualifying reasons.</p><h3 id=\"texas-sales-tax-rate-2025-vs-2026\">Texas Sales Tax Rate 2025 vs 2026</h3><p>The Texas sales tax rate 2025 was 6.25% (state), and it remains 6.25% for 2026. The state base rate rarely changes, but local rates can shift based on voter-approved measures. Check the texas sales tax chart from the Comptroller for the most current rates by jurisdiction.</p><h2 id=\"frequently-asked-questions-about-texas-taxes\">Frequently Asked Questions About Texas Taxes</h2><h3 id=\"does-texas-have-income-tax\">Does Texas have income tax?</h3><p>No. Texas is one of nine states with no personal income tax. The Texas income tax rate is 0% for all individuals, regardless of income level. This is protected by the Texas Constitution, making it extremely difficult to change.</p><h3 id=\"what-is-the-income-tax-rate-in-texas\">What is the income tax rate in Texas?</h3><p>The income tax rate in Texas is 0%. There are no texas income tax brackets for individuals. The only income-like tax is the franchise tax on businesses, which ranges from 0.331% to 0.75% on business margins.</p><h3 id=\"how-much-is-100-000-taxed-in-texas\">How much is $100,000 taxed in Texas?</h3><p>On $100,000 in Texas (single filer), you pay approximately $14,001 in federal income tax and $7,649 in FICA. Texas state income tax is $0. Your total tax is about $21,650, leaving you with ~$78,350 before property and sales taxes.</p><h3 id=\"is-tax-8-25-in-texas\">Is tax 8.25% in Texas?</h3><p>The maximum combined sales tax rate in Texas is 8.25% (6.25% state + 2% local). Not all areas charge the full 8.25% — some have lower rates. Major cities like Dallas, Houston, and Austin typically charge the full 8.25%.</p><h3 id=\"how-much-tax-is-on-100-in-texas\">How much tax is on $100 in Texas?</h3><p>On a $100 purchase in Texas at the standard 8.25% combined rate, you'd pay $8.25 in sales tax, for a total of $108.25. In areas with lower local rates, the tax would be less (e.g., $6.25 at the state-only rate).</p><h3 id=\"what-is-the-sales-tax-on-a-30-000-car-in-texas\">What is the sales tax on a $30,000 car in Texas?</h3><p>The sales tax on a $30,000 car in Texas is $1,875 (6.25% state rate only — no local add-ons for motor vehicles). This is significantly lower than the general sales tax rate in most Texas cities.</p><h3 id=\"how-do-texas-taxes-compare-to-other-states\">How do Texas taxes compare to other states?</h3><p>Texas has a lower overall tax burden (~8.22%) compared to California (~11.49%), New York (~12.7%), and Illinois (~11.0%). However, Texas property taxes are among the highest in the nation. Use our <a href=\"/compare\">comparison tools</a> for detailed state-by-state analysis.</p></div></div></div><div class=\"mx-auto max-w-3xl mt-8\"><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors\" href=\"/blog\">← Back to Blog</a></div></article><section aria-label=\"Author: Rachel Mitchell\" class=\"rounded-xl border border-emerald-500/20 bg-gradient-to-br from-emerald-500/5 to-card/80 p-5 sm:p-6\"><div class=\"flex items-start gap-4\"><img src=\"https://thetaxcalc.com/author-rachel-mitchell.webp\" alt=\"Rachel Mitchell, CPA\" width=\"48\" height=\"48\" class=\"h-12 w-12 shrink-0 rounded-full object-cover ring-1 ring-emerald-500/30\" loading=\"lazy\"/><div class=\"min-w-0 flex-1\"><div class=\"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2\"><a class=\"text-base font-semibold text-foreground hover:text-emerald-400 transition-colors\" href=\"/about#rachel-mitchell\">Rachel Mitchell</a><span class=\"inline-flex items-center rounded-md bg-emerald-500/15 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-semibold text-emerald-400 ring-1 ring-emerald-500/25\">CPA</span></div><p class=\"mt-0.5 text-sm text-muted-foreground\">Lead Tax Analyst &amp; Editorial Director<!-- -->, TheTaxCalc</p><p class=\"mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground line-clamp-2\">Rachel Mitchell is a Certified Public Accountant (CPA) licensed in Illinois with over 12 years of experience in individual and small-business taxation. She specializes in federal and state income tax compliance, FICA optimization, payroll tax strategy, and multi-state tax planning. Rachel holds an MS in Taxation from Golden Gate University and a BS in Accounting from the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign. She is an active member of the American Institute of Certified Public Accountants (AICPA) and the Illinois CPA Society. Before joining TheTaxCalc, Rachel spent 8 years at a Big Four accounting firm advising high-net-worth clients on tax-efficient wealth strategies.</p><div class=\"mt-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-1 text-xs text-muted-foreground\"><span class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1\"><svg class=\"h-3 w-3 text-emerald-500/60\" fill=\"none\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" d=\"M6.75 3v2.25M17.25 3v2.25M3 18.75V7.5a2.25 2.25 0 012.25-2.25h13.5A2.25 2.25 0 0121 7.5v11.25m-18 0A2.25 2.25 0 005.25 21h13.5A2.25 2.25 0 0021 18.75m-18 0v-7.5A2.25 2.25 0 015.25 9h13.5A2.25 2.25 0 0121 11.25v7.5\"></path></svg>Reviewed: January 2026</span><span>Tax data verified against IRS Publication 15-T &amp; state revenue departments</span></div></div></div></section><section class=\"mb-8 rounded-xl border border-emerald-500/20 bg-emerald-500/5 p-6\"><h2 class=\"text-lg font-bold text-foreground mb-3\">Try Our Tax Calculators</h2><p class=\"text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-4\">See exactly how much you&#x27;ll take home after all taxes and deductions.</p><div class=\"flex flex-wrap gap-3\"><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-600 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-white hover:bg-emerald-700 transition-colors\" href=\"/paycheck-calculator\">Paycheck Calculator</a><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-border/50 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/30 transition-colors\" href=\"/illinois-tax-calculator\">IL Calculator</a><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-border/50 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/30 transition-colors\" href=\"/texas-tax-calculator\">TX Calculator</a><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-border/50 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/30 transition-colors\" href=\"/florida-tax-calculator\">FL Calculator</a><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-border/50 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/30 transition-colors\" href=\"/self-employment-tax-calculator\">Self-Employment</a><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-border/50 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/30 transition-colors\" href=\"/relocation-calculator\">Compare States</a><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-border/50 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/30 transition-colors\" href=\"/salary\">Salary Calculator</a><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-border/50 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/30 transition-colors\" href=\"/compare\">Compare Salaries</a><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-border/50 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/30 transition-colors\" href=\"/glossary\">Tax Glossary</a><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-border/50 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/30 transition-colors\" href=\"/federal-tax-brackets\">Federal Tax Brackets</a></div></section><section class=\"mb-8 rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-muted/30 p-6\"><h2 class=\"text-lg font-bold text-foreground mb-3\">Popular Salary Calculations</h2><p class=\"text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-4\">Quick access to take-home pay estimates for common salary levels.</p><div class=\"flex flex-wrap gap-3\"><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-emerald-500/20 bg-emerald-500/5 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/40 hover:bg-emerald-500/10 transition-colors\" href=\"/salary/50000\">$50,000</a><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-emerald-500/20 bg-emera